have  been in our clinical trials in terms of  the injections both of the drugs that  are available now have self injections  pens so they're easy for patients to use  there can be some local skin reactions  but the seems to be much lower than  initially anticipated so I found that  that our patients are tolerating it well  they're not having problems with  self-injection and I haven't gotten a  lot of pushback great thank you for that  answer we have another question from an  audience member would it be lowest  dosage of statin that can be given well  you can give any any goes you like I  guess but but it's recommended that you  give a dose of a statin that can lower  LDL cholesterol at least 30% if possible  so any any of the dose ranges of receive  a statin and atorvastatin would meet  that 40 to 80 milligrams of simvastatin  80 milligrams of pravastatin for example  our drugs that would meet that criteria  you know and that's based on the  clinical trials that have been done and  most of the trials that have been done  in the past have used those doses of  drugs or higher to achieve 30 to 50  percent or more reductions so that's  where that that comes from but sometimes  we use lower doses because they can't  tolerate higher doses and that's a  situation where we would often consider  using other therapies like nice and  Rosetta might in combination in the  high-risk patient group okay thank you  for that we are almost out of time but  we do have time for one last question  any comments on PFC k9 inhibitors along  with statins do we give a highest dose  or low dose yeah well I mean this is  something that's still evolving we do  know that whether you use a pcsk9 as  mono therapy or in addition to statins  it has the same effect about a 50%  reduction in LDL cholesterol the problem  is we don't have the outcome data at the  moment so and most of the pcsk9 drugs  have very little in terms of titration  so if you're going to add a pcsk9 with a  LaRocca map you can titrate from 75 to  150 but you're still going to see  relative high reductions in LDL  cholesterol and with Eva loca  it's a single-dose so I think the more  important question is when you started  in patients that are on sentence so  should all patients that have LDL  cholesterol zuv 80 or 90 in a high-risk  group that are on maximally tolerated  statins we placed it on this drug I  think at the moment we know it'll lower  the cholesterol another 50% but we don't  really have a lot of good outcome data I  think I tend to be at the moment leaning  toward those individuals that are very  high-risk that have LDL cholesterol that  are above 100 or patients that we still  have high LDL cholesterol that our  statin intolerant patients that have had  recurrent events despite being on  adequate statin therapy and individuals  with familial hypercholesterolemia we're  even on maximal statins LDL cholesterol  are still high even a primary care  population but I think this is going to  be sorted out  more over time as the long term outcome  trials are done I mean there's also the  controversy at the other end can we get  too low with LDL cholesterol so far in  these trials we haven't seen a strong  signal that very low LDL cholesterol are  associated with a higher incidence of  adverse events but we'll have a lot more  information from the the long randomized  trials great thank you that is all the  time we have for our questions I'd like  to thank Michael Rocco once again for  his presentation and just ask him if he  has any final comments to leave our  audience with no I I thank you for being  here and listening I think the important  thing to remember with guidelines is  that they're just that they're  guidelines they're not rules  we can apply these to our individual  patients but we always have to look at  the patient as an individual and make  the best decision for that individual  patient these guidelines are just there  to aid us in starting these  conversations and 